@node	1.3.2, 1.3.3, 1.3.1, 1.3
@subsection Constructing Procedures Using @code{Lambda}

In using @code{sum} as in @ref{1.3.1}, it seems terribly awkward to
have to define trivial procedures such as @code{pi-term} and @code{pi-next}
just so we can use them as arguments to our higher-order procedure.  Rather
than define @code{pi-next} and @code{pi-term}, it would be more convenient to
have a way to directly specify ``the procedure that returns its input
incremented by 4'' and ``the procedure that returns the reciprocal of its input
times its input plus 2.''  The primer introduced the closure expression for
exactly this: a procedure written where it is used, which captures what it
needs from the surrounding scope.  Using closures we can describe what we want as

@example rust
|x: f64| x + 4.0
@end example

@noindent
and

@example rust
|x: f64| 1.0 / (x * (x + 2.0))
@end example

@noindent
Then our @code{pi-sum} procedure can be expressed without defining any
auxiliary procedures as

@example rust
fn pi_sum(a: f64, b: f64) -> f64 @{
    sum(&|x| 1.0 / (x * (x + 2.0)), a, &|x| x + 4.0, b)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Again using a closure, we can write the @code{integral} procedure without
having to define the auxiliary procedure @code{add-dx}:

@example rust
fn integral(f: &dyn Fn(f64) -> f64, a: f64, b: f64, dx: f64) -> f64 @{
    sum(f, a + dx / 2.0, &|x| x + dx, b) * dx
@}
@end example

@noindent
In general, a closure expression creates a procedure in the same way as a
@code{fn} definition, except that no name is specified for the procedure:

@example rust
|@code{⟨}@var{parameters}@code{⟩}| @code{⟨}@var{body}@code{⟩}
@end example

@noindent
The resulting procedure is just as much a procedure as one that is created
by a @code{fn} definition.  The only difference is that it has not been
associated with any name in the environment.  In fact,

@example rust
fn plus4(x: f64) -> f64 @{
    x + 4.0
@}
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example rust
let plus4 = |x: f64| x + 4.0;
@end example

@noindent
We can read a closure expression as follows:

@example
|x: f64|      x + 4.0
    |            |
the procedure of an argument x that adds x and 4
@end example

@noindent
Like any expression that has a procedure as its value, a closure
expression can be used as the operator in a call such as

@example rust
println!("@{@}", (|x: f64, y: f64, z: f64| x + y + square(z))(1.0, 2.0, 3.0));
// => 12
@end example

@noindent
or, more generally, in any context where we would normally use a procedure
name.@footnote{Rust borrows the shape of its closure syntax from the
λ-calculus, a mathematical formalism introduced by the mathematical logician
Alonzo @ref{Church (1941)}.  Church developed the λ-calculus to provide a
rigorous foundation for studying the notions of function and function
application.  The λ-calculus has become a basic tool for mathematical
investigations of the semantics of programming languages.}

@subsubheading Using @code{let} to create local variables

Another use of closures is in creating local variables.  We often need
local variables in our procedures other than those that have been bound as
formal parameters.  For example, suppose we wish to compute the function
@displaymath
{f(x,y)} \,=\, {x(1 + xy)^2} + {y(1 - y)} + {(1 + xy)(1 - y),}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
f(x,y) = x(1 + xy)^2 + y(1 - y) + (1 + xy)(1 - y)
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
which we could also express as
@displaymath
\eqalign{
  a                     &=   {1 + xy,}  \cr
  \hphantom{(x,y)} b    &=   {1 - y,}   \cr
  {f(x,y)}              &=   {xa^2} + {yb} + {ab.}
}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
     a = 1 + xy
     b = 1 - y
f(x,y) = xa^2 + yb + ab
@end example

@end ifinfo
In writing a procedure to compute @math{f}, we would like to include as local
variables not only @math{x} and @math{y} but also the names of intermediate
quantities like @math{a} and @math{b}.  One way to accomplish this is to use a
nested procedure that takes the local names as parameters:

@example rust
fn f_helper(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 @{
    fn f_from_locals(a: f64, b: f64, x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 @{
        x * square(a) + y * b + a * b
    @}
    f_from_locals(1.0 + x * y, 1.0 - y, x, y)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Of course, we could use a closure expression to specify an anonymous
procedure for binding our local variables.  The body of @code{f} then becomes a
single call to that procedure:

@example rust
fn f_closure(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 @{
    (|a: f64, b: f64| x * square(a) + y * b + a * b)(1.0 + x * y, 1.0 - y)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Rust's binding form makes the same convenience direct.  Using @code{let},
the @code{f} procedure could be written as

@example rust
fn f_let(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 @{
    let a = 1.0 + x * y;
    let b = 1.0 - y;
    x * square(a) + y * b + a * b
@}
@end example

@noindent
The general form of a @code{let} binding is

@example rust
let @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} = @code{⟨}@var{expression}@code{⟩};
@end example

@noindent
which can be thought of as saying

@example
let ⟨@var{name₁}⟩ @r{have the value} ⟨@var{exp₁}⟩ @r{and}
    ⟨@var{name₂}⟩ @r{have the value} ⟨@var{exp₂}⟩ @r{and}
    @r{…}
  @r{in} ⟨@var{body}⟩
@end example

@noindent
Each name is associated with the value of the corresponding expression, and the
rest of the block is evaluated with these names bound as local variables.
Here the two languages part company.  Scheme's @code{let} is an alternate
syntax for applying an anonymous procedure: every value is computed
@emph{before} any of the new names exists.  Rust's @code{let} is an ordinary
binding that takes effect where it stands, and a second @code{let} using the
same name @newterm{shadows} the first from that point on.  No special
mechanism is needed to provide local variables: a @code{let} binding is an
ordinary binding whose scope ends at the end of its block.

We can see from this difference that the scope of a variable specified by a
@code{let} binding runs from the binding to the end of the block.  Two
consequences:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Let} allows one to bind variables as locally as possible to where they
are to be used.  For example, if the value of @code{x} is 5, the value of the
expression

@example rust
@{
    let x = 3.0;
    x + x * 10.0
@} + x
@end example

@noindent
is 38.  Here, the @code{x} in the block is 3, so the value of the block
is 33.  On the other hand, the @code{x} that is the second operand of the
outer @code{+} is still 5.

@item
A Rust @code{let} shadows immediately, where Scheme's @code{let} computes all
its values outside the new bindings.  This matters when the expressions that
provide the values for the local variables depend upon variables having the
same names as the local variables themselves.  For example, if the value of
@code{x} is 2, Scheme evaluates @code{(let ((x 3) (y (+ x 2))) (* x y))} as
follows: the value of @code{y} is computed from the outer @code{x}, so
@code{y} is 4 and the whole expression is 12.  The same two lines in Rust

@example rust
let x = 2.0;
let x = 3.0;
let y = x + 2.0;
println!("@{@}", x * y);
// => 15
@end example

@noindent
have the value 15, because by the time @code{y} is computed the name
@code{x} has already been shadowed and is 3.

@end itemize

@noindent
Sometimes a nested definition can get the same effect as a @code{let}.  For
values, though, Rust needs no second spelling: the @code{let} bindings
@emph{are} the local definitions, and a nested @code{fn} definition serves for
local procedures.  We prefer, therefore, to use @code{let} for local values
and nested @code{fn} definitions only for internal procedures.@footnote{Understanding
internal definitions well enough to be sure a program means what we intend it
to mean requires a more elaborate model of the evaluation process than we have
presented in this chapter.  The subtleties do not arise with internal
definitions of procedures, however.  We will return to this issue in
@ref{4.1.6}, after we learn more about evaluation.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.34}Exercise 1.34:} Suppose we define the procedure

@example rust
fn f(g: fn(f64) -> f64) -> f64 @{
    g(2.0)
@}
@end example

Then we have

@example rust
println!("@{@}", f(square));
// => 4

println!("@{@}", f(|z| z * (z + 1.0)));
// => 6
@end example

What happens if we (perversely) pass @code{f} to itself and write
@code{f(f)}?  Try it, read the error the compiler reports, and explain what
the call would do at run time in a language that accepted it.
@end quotation

@node	1.3.3, 1.3.4, 1.3.2, 1.3
@subsection Procedures as General Methods

We introduced compound procedures in @ref{1.1.4} as a mechanism for
abstracting patterns of numerical operations so as to make them independent of
the particular numbers involved.  With higher-order procedures, such as the
@code{integral} procedure of @ref{1.3.1}, we began to see a more
powerful kind of abstraction: procedures used to express general methods of
computation, independent of the particular functions involved.  In this section
we discuss two more elaborate examples---general methods for finding zeros and
fixed points of functions---and show how these methods can be expressed
directly as procedures.

@subsubheading Finding roots of equations by the half-interval method

The @newterm{half-interval method} is a simple but powerful technique for
finding roots of an equation @math{{f(x) = 0}}, where @math{f} is a continuous
function.  The idea is that, if we are given points @math{a} and @math{b} such that
@math{{f(a) < 0 < f(b)}}, then @math{f} must have at least one zero between
@math{a} and @math{b}.  To locate a zero, let @math{x} be the average of @math{a} and @math{b},
and compute @math{{f(x)}}.  If @math{{f(x) > 0}}, then @math{f} must have a zero
between @math{a} and @math{x}.  If @math{{f(x) < 0}}, then @math{f} must have a zero
between @math{x} and @math{b}.  Continuing in this way, we can identify smaller and
smaller intervals on which @math{f} must have a zero.  When we reach a point where
the interval is small enough, the process stops.  Since the interval of
uncertainty is reduced by half at each step of the process, the number of steps
required grows as @math{{\Theta(\log(L\, /\, T))}}, where @math{L} is the
length of the original interval and @math{T} is the error tolerance (that is, the
size of the interval we will consider ``small enough'').  Here is a procedure
that implements this strategy:

@example rust
fn search(f: &dyn Fn(f64) -> f64, neg_point: f64, pos_point: f64) -> f64 @{
    let midpoint = average(neg_point, pos_point);
    if close_enough(neg_point, pos_point) @{
        midpoint
    @} else @{
        let test_value = f(midpoint);
        if test_value > 0.0 @{
            search(f, neg_point, midpoint)
        @} else if test_value < 0.0 @{
            search(f, midpoint, pos_point)
        @} else @{
            midpoint
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
We assume that we are initially given the function @math{f} together with points
at which its values are negative and positive.  We first compute the midpoint
of the two given points.  Next we check to see if the given interval is small
enough, and if so we simply return the midpoint as our answer.  Otherwise, we
compute as a test value the value of @math{f} at the midpoint.  If the test value
is positive, then we continue the process with a new interval running from the
original negative point to the midpoint.  If the test value is negative, we
continue with the interval from the midpoint to the positive point.  Finally,
there is the possibility that the test value is 0, in which case the midpoint
is itself the root we are searching for.

To test whether the endpoints are ``close enough'' we can use a procedure
similar to the one used in @ref{1.1.7} for computing square
roots:@footnote{We have used 0.001 as a representative ``small'' number to
indicate a tolerance for the acceptable error in a calculation.  The
appropriate tolerance for a real calculation depends upon the problem to be
solved and the limitations of the computer and the algorithm.  This is often a
very subtle consideration, requiring help from a numerical analyst or some
other kind of magician.}

@example rust
fn close_enough(x: f64, y: f64) -> bool @{
    (x - y).abs() < 0.001
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Search} is awkward to use directly, because we can accidentally give it
points at which @math{f}'s values do not have the required sign, in which case we
get a wrong answer.  Instead we will use @code{search} via the following
procedure, which checks to see which of the endpoints has a negative function
value and which has a positive value, and calls the @code{search} procedure
accordingly.  If the function has the same sign on the two given points, the
half-interval method cannot be used, in which case the procedure signals an
error.@footnote{The book's @code{error} prints its arguments as a message and
aborts.  In this edition the failure is a value too: the error half of the
@code{Result} below carries the message and the two offending points, and the
caller decides what to do with it.}

@example rust
fn half_interval_method(
    f: impl Fn(f64) -> f64,
    a: f64,
    b: f64,
) -> Result<f64, SchemeError> @{
    let a_value = f(a);
    let b_value = f(b);
    if a_value < 0.0 && b_value > 0.0 @{
        Ok(search(&f, a, b))
    @} else if b_value < 0.0 && a_value > 0.0 @{
        Ok(search(&f, b, a))
    @} else @{
        Err(SchemeError::UserRaised @{
            message: "Values are not of opposite sign".to_owned(),
            irritants: vec![Value::Real(a), Value::Real(b)],
        @})
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The following example uses the half-interval method to approximate @math{\pi} as
the root between 2 and 4 of @math{{\sin x = 0}}:

@example rust
match half_interval_method(f64::sin, 2.0, 4.0) @{
    Ok(pi_approx) => println!("@{@}", pi_approx),
    Err(err) => println!("Error: @{err@}"),
@}
// => 3.14111328125
@end example

@noindent
Here is another example, using the half-interval method to search for a root of
the equation @math{{x^3 - 2x - 3 = 0}} between 1 and 2:

@example rust
match half_interval_method(|x| x * x * x - 2.0 * x - 3.0, 1.0, 2.0) @{
    Ok(root) => println!("@{@}", root),
    Err(err) => println!("Error: @{err@}"),
@}
// => 1.89306640625
@end example

@subsubheading Finding fixed points of functions

A number @math{x} is called a @newterm{fixed point} of a function @math{f} if @math{x}
satisfies the equation @math{{f(x) = x}}.  For some functions @math{f} we can
locate a fixed point by beginning with an initial guess and applying @math{f}
repeatedly,
@displaymath
{f(x),}\quad {f(f(x)),}\quad {f(f(f(x))),} \quad{\dots,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
f(x), f(f(x)), f(f(f(x))), ...
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
until the value does not change very much.  Using this idea, we can devise a
procedure @code{fixed_point} that takes as inputs a function and an initial
guess and produces an approximation to a fixed point of the function.  We apply
the function repeatedly until we find two successive values whose difference is
less than some prescribed tolerance:

@example rust
const TOLERANCE: f64 = 0.00001;

fn fixed_point(f: impl Fn(f64) -> f64, first_guess: f64) -> f64 @{
    fn close_enough(v1: f64, v2: f64) -> bool @{
        (v1 - v2).abs() < TOLERANCE
    @}
    fn try_guess(f: &dyn Fn(f64) -> f64, guess: f64) -> f64 @{
        let next = f(guess);
        if close_enough(guess, next) @{
            next
        @} else @{
            try_guess(f, next)
        @}
    @}
    try_guess(&f, first_guess)
@}
@end example

@noindent
For example, we can use this method to approximate the fixed point of the
cosine function, starting with 1 as an initial approximation:@footnote{Try this
during a boring lecture: Set your calculator to radians mode and then
repeatedly press the @code{cos} button until you obtain the fixed point.}

@example rust
println!("@{@}", fixed_point(f64::cos, 1.0));
// => 0.7390822985224024
@end example

@noindent
Similarly, we can find a solution to the equation
@math{{y = \sin y + \cos y}}:

@example rust
println!("@{@}", fixed_point(|y| y.sin() + y.cos(), 1.0));
// => 1.2587315962971173
@end example

@noindent
The fixed-point process is reminiscent of the process we used for finding
square roots in @ref{1.1.7}.  Both are based on the idea of repeatedly
improving a guess until the result satisfies some criterion.  In fact, we can
readily formulate the square-root computation as a fixed-point search.
Computing the square root of some number @math{x} requires finding a @math{y} such
that @math{{y^2 = x}}.  Putting this equation into the equivalent form
@math{{y = x / y}}, we recognize that we are looking for a fixed point of the
function@footnote{@math{\mapsto} (pronounced ``maps to'') is the mathematician's way of
writing a closure.  @math{{y \mapsto x / y}} means @code{|y| x / y},
that is, the function whose value at @math{y} is @math{{x / y}}.} @math{{y \mapsto x / y}},
and we can therefore try to compute square roots as

@example rust
fn sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 @{
    fixed_point(move |y| x / y, 1.0)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Unfortunately, this fixed-point search does not converge.  Consider an initial
guess @math{y_1}.  The next guess is @math{{y_2 = x / y_1}} and the next guess is
@math{y_3 = {x / y_2} = {x / (x / y_1)} = y_1}.  This results in an
infinite loop in which the two guesses @math{y_1} and @math{y_2} repeat over and
over, oscillating about the answer.

One way to control such oscillations is to prevent the guesses from changing so
much.  Since the answer is always between our guess @math{y} and @math{{x / y}}, we
can make a new guess that is not as far from @math{y} as @math{{x / y}} by averaging
@math{y} with @math{{x / y}}, so that the next guess after @math{y} is
@math{{{1\over2}(y + x / y)}}
instead of @math{{x / y}}.  The process of making such a sequence of
guesses is simply the process of looking for a fixed point of
@math{y \mapsto {{1\over2}(y + x / y)}}:

@example rust
fn sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 @{
    fixed_point(move |y| average(y, x / y), 1.0)
@}
@end example

@noindent
(Note that @math{y = {{1\over2}(y + x / y)}} is a simple transformation of the
equation @math{{y = x / y;}} to derive it, add @math{y} to both sides of the
equation and divide by 2.  The @code{move} in front of each closure says that
the closure takes ownership of what it captures (here the @code{x} the
procedure was called with), which is Rust's price for letting a procedure
outlive the call that made it.)

With this modification, the square-root procedure works.  In fact, if we
unravel the definitions, we can see that the sequence of approximations to the
square root generated here is precisely the same as the one generated by our
original square-root procedure of @ref{1.1.7}.  This approach of
averaging successive approximations to a solution, a technique that we call
@newterm{average damping}, often aids the convergence of fixed-point searches.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.35}Exercise 1.35:} Show that the golden ratio
@math{\varphi} (@ref{1.2.2}) is a fixed point of the transformation
@math{{x \mapsto 1 + 1 / x}}, and use this fact to compute @math{\varphi} by means
of the @code{fixed_point} function.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.36}Exercise 1.36:} Modify @code{fixed_point} so that
it prints the sequence of approximations it generates, one guess per line, the
way the primality listings of @ref{Exercise 1.22} print.  Then find a
solution to @math{{x^x = 1000}} by finding a fixed point of @math{x \mapsto
{\log(1000) / \log(x)}}.  (Rust's @code{f64::ln} computes natural
logarithms.)  Compare the number of steps
this takes with and without average damping.  (Note that you cannot start
@code{fixed_point} with a guess of 1, as this would cause division by
@math{{\log(1) = 0}}.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.37}Exercise 1.37:}

